I finally hacked the PGSI into REND386 (actually did this about two
weeks ago, but had to leave on what turned out to be a two-week
business trip). 

I've posted this to sunee.uwaterloo.ca under
pub/vr/incoming/devel5.zip. 

Basically the same as devel4.zip with the following caveats; also,
I've included the READ.ME  file from the .ZIP. 

1) This is DEFINITELY a hacked version. I used IBMCOM.C (sent to 
   me by Chris Hand) which replaces the com port interrupt routine
   in order to keep up with 9600 baud from the PGSI. 

   It does NOT back out gracefully; so if you run rend386 and then
   try to run mouse-based stuff (such as WINDOZE) then you'll
   DEFINITELY get some weird crashes. 
2) If folks look at the code, they'll see that I put in
   code to "sync" with the PGSI data stream. I know this is wasteful, 
   but I don't have access to a serial port datascope and this works
   consistently (at least on my machine). 
3) I developed this on a 486/40MHZ machine, so if it is unbearably
   slow I apologize in advance. 
4) I just today got word that the PGSI test code was posted; I have 
   downloaded it but haven't looked at it yet. 
+5) My code does NOT support the SEGA glasses at present.
   Problem is/was that adding the interrupt-driven PowerGlove support
   required me to rip out the SEGA handling code in order to fit into the
   brain-damaged DOS memory requirements.

Have fun! Let's get this sucker doing some really cool stuff, like
using the glove arrows instead of keyboard arrows to move around, etc.
etc. 

Charlie
----------------READ.ME file from DEVEL5.ZIP-----------------------
7/29/93

Contents of this PKZIP file (produced by pkzip 2.03): 

1) Fixes to WORLD.C to allow it to compile under BORLAND C++ version
   2.0 (already compiles OK under 3.1). I replaced about 80% of the 
   if/then/else/if/then/else/if/then .. block with a correspondingly
   huge SELECT statement. 
2) Modified the makefiles to invoke BCC instead of TCC (Turbo C). 
3) Also modified the makefiles to append ".c" to the end of the
   names of source files (this to make the BCC command properly 
   compile them as C code instead of C++). 
4) Threw in mods from Chris Hand for support of the Menelli box
   (see read.me in the SEGA subdirectory for details). 

I'm currently hacking hard to get the PGSI (PowerGlove Serial Port) to work
with REND386 (starting with Chris' code). 

